\begin_layout Standard
A quantum circuit is a visual and mathematical representation of a quantum computation,
 analogous to an electronic circuit in classical computing.
 It consists of a sequence of quantum gates,
 measurements,
 and sometimes resets,
 applied to a set of qubits over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The qubits are usually depicted as horizontal lines,
 each representing one two level quantum system.
 Time flows from left to right.
 A gate acting on a qubit is shown as a box or symbol placed on the qubit’s line at the appropriate time;
 multi-qubit gates (like CNOT or SWAP) connect several lines.
 The gates implement unitary transformations on the state of the qubits.
 Measurement gates,
 often shown as a meter symbol,
 collapse the quantum state to a classical outcome 0 or 1.
\end_layout

