\begin_layout Standard
When a circuit is executed on a quantum computer,
 the initial state (usually |0...0⟩) is transformed by each gate in sequence,
 and finally the qubits are measured,
 producing a string of classical bits.
 Because of superposition and entanglement,
 the output of a circuit is generally probabilistic;
 multiple runs (shots) are taken to estimate the distribution of results.
\end_layout

