\begin_layout Standard
In the image you can see 4 qubits (q0,
 q1,
 q2,
 q3,
 q4),
 each put into equal to |0⟩ at the starting position.
 The H (Hadamard) gate on each qubit means that,
 before measurement,
 every one of the 4 qubits has a 50/50 chance of coming out 0 or 1 and this is the source of the 
\emph on
quantum randomness
\emph default
 that I have tried to add to the whole game to generate more interesting visualizations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One entangling gate cx(1,
 0),
 represented in the image as a blue circle with a plus in the middle that connect the q0 to q1,
 is a CNOT.
 The qubit 1 acts as the controller and qubit 0 as the target.
 If qubit 1 measures 1,
 qubit 0 value gets flipped;
 if qubit 1 measures 0,
 qubit 0 is left alone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All 4 qubits measured simultaneously,
 giving a random 4 bit string used to build one card:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
qubit 0 bit → give a the sign to the card,
 positive or negative (+ or -)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
qubits 1,
 2,
 3 bits → combined into a 3 bit number (0 - 7) → picks which emoji out of the 8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This whole circuit runs once per card,
 discarding duplicates,
 until all 16 distinct sign and emoji combinations exist.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the deck generation,
 the last thing that happens in the circuit is the 
\emph on
measurement
\emph default
.
 This operation is destructive in quantum mechanics;
 the instant a qubit is measured,
 its superposition collapses into one definite classical value (0 or 1),
 and that quantum information is lost.
 After that the Python code consider only a plain 4 bit string (like 0011);
 not a quantum state anymore but only four classical bits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So during the visualization of the popup after match,
 the source code builds a fresh 2 qubit circuit from scratch,
 starting clean at |00⟩,
 and uses the matched card's sign and emoji identity only as classical inputs that decide which gates to apply depending to the experiment and not as some inherited quantum state.
 That's what makes it possible to have a controlled,
 reproducible,
 exactly known state to actually show you:
 real superposition,
 real entanglement (or deliberately none,
 for Experiment 1),
 still fully intact right up until the moment the popup renders it.
\end_layout

