\begin_layout Standard
The circuit associated with this experiment comprises a single qubit R
\begin_inset Formula $_{y}$
\end_inset

 rotation applied to the first qubit,
 followed by a controlled NOT gate (in the picture the cross inside the blue circle) with the first qubit as control and the second as target.
 Two additional single qubit NOT gates may be conditionally applied to the first and second qubit respectively,
 according to the sign encoded on the corresponding cards;
 these operations,
 being local,
 do not affect the degree of entanglement of the resulting state,
 but determine which of the two computational basis states is associated with a positive or negative amplitude.
 The rotation angle preceding the controlled NOT gate is obtained through a quantum random process and is fixed independently for each pictorial symbol.
 As this angle varies,
 the resulting two qubit state exhibits an entanglement entropy ranging continuously between zero and one ebit,
 with the maximal value attained when the rotation angle equals π/2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This experiment is designed to illustrate that 
\emph on
entanglement admits intermediate degrees
\emph default
,
 in contrast to the binary distinction addressed in the preceding experiment,
 and to demonstrate the corresponding progressive reduction in the definiteness of the individual qubit states.
\end_layout

