\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The circuit associated with this experiment consists of a Hadamard gate applied to the first qubit,
 followed by a controlled NOT gate with the first qubit as control and the second as target,
 generating a 
\emph on
maximally entangled state
\emph default
.
 A Z gate may be conditionally applied to the first qubit according to the sign of the corresponding card,
 and an X gate may be conditionally applied to the second qubit according to the parity of the index associated with the pictorial symbol.
 The combined application of these two conditional operations 
\emph on
allows the circuit to generate any one of the four Bell states
\emph default
.
 Each of these four states possesses identical entanglement entropy,
 equal to one ebit,
 and identical marginal probability distributions for the individual qubits,
 yet they are mutually distinguishable through the relative phase and bit-parity structure of their amplitudes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This experiment demonstrates that 
\emph on
maximal entanglement does not correspond to a single quantum state
\emph default
,
 but to an equivalence class of states requiring additional structural information for their complete characterization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As we have seen before,
 the Bell states can be obtained with different circuits,
 but in this experiment we are demonstrating that also with the circuit producing Φ⁺ we can reach all of the other three Bell states,
 Φ⁻,
 Ψ⁺,
 and Ψ⁻,
 without changing the entangling step itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To be more precise,
 we are using a 
\emph on
Bell state preparation circuit
\emph default
 (Hadamard followed by CNOT),
 circuit that produces Φ⁺;
 followed by a 
\emph on
conditional Pauli encoding
\emph default
 step (Z and/or X,
 applied to one qubit only).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The idea behind this experiment is that,
 once two qubits have been made entangled,
 moving between the four Bell states does not require building the entanglement again from scratch.
 Starting from the same entangled pair,
 a small operation performed on only one of the two qubits,
 leaving the other completely untouched,
 is enough to turn the pair into a different member of the Bell family.
 In this experiment,
 that operation is decided by two pieces of information that the game already provides:
 the sign of the matched card,
 which decides whether a phase adjustment is introduced,
 and the identity of the matched picture,
 which decides whether a bit flip is introduced.
 Depending on which of these two conditions are met,
 the same starting entangled pair ends up as one of the four possible Bell states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What makes this worth highlighting from an educational standpoint is twofold.
 First,
 it shows that maximal entanglement does not describe a single,
 unique state of two qubits,
 but rather an entire family of four states,
 all equally and maximally entangled,
 and distinguished from each other only by these small,
 local adjustments.
 Second,
 and more strikingly,
 it shows that acting on just one of the two qubits is sufficient to change which member of that family the pair becomes,
 even though the two qubits remain entangled throughout,
 and even though the second qubit is never directly touched.
 This has no real counterpart in classical physics,
 where performing an operation on one object cannot change how that object is jointly described together with another,
 distant object.
 It is precisely this property,
 of steering a shared entangled pair by acting on only one half of it,
 that underlies well-known quantum communication protocols such as superdense coding,
 in which a sender is able to communicate one of four possible messages to a receiver by transmitting a single qubit,
 relying on an entangled pair the two parties already share in advance.
\end_layout

